Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.3. Board's Decision Making Process. <omitted> (c) At any time prior to the Company's Stockholder Approval, the Company Board may engage in discussions or negotiations with a third party that has made a bona fide acquisition proposal if the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel, that the failure to engage in such discussions or negotiations would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law. (d) The Company Board shall, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized reputation, determine whether such acquisition proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal. In making such determination, the Company Board shall take into account all legal, strategic, and financial aspects of the proposal, as well as the potential impact on the Company's stakeholders. (e) If the Company Board determines that the proposal is a Superior Proposal, it shall provide Parent with a written notice of its intent to change its recommendation or terminate this Agreement. The Company shall allow Parent a period of five Business Days to make adjustments to the terms of this Agreement so that the acquisition proposal ceases to be a Superior Proposal. (Pages 89-91)\nAnswer:
Choices:

